llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232), on top of
patches 108 and 112: the MoE expert prefetch of patch 108 is only two
splits deep, and with host-blocking HIP waits the GPU idles between expert
splits. Not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Patch 108 copies a MoE split's host expert weights into one of two device
slots while the previous split computes. The scheduler splits a MoE layer
at every weight kept in another buffer, so a Qwen3.6 35B-A3B layer is
three prefetched splits (gate, up, down; one expert tensor each, 144 MiB
Q4_K or 210 MiB Q6_K), and the two slots alternate per split: the copy for
split k+1 goes into the slot of split k-1 and waits for it.

The copies already run at the link limit (13.3 GB/s on PCIe Gen3 x16,
1.47 s of copies per 2048-token batch). The loss is in the scheduling.
ROC_CPU_WAIT_FOR_SIGNAL=1, which gfx8 needs so that no wait packet lands
in the SDMA queue, makes HIP wait for events on the host: the scheduler
thread blocks in hipStreamWaitEvent until split k-1 has finished and then
in hipEventRecord on the copy stream for the whole copy, and only then
builds and submits split k+1. The GPU queue never holds more than one
split, and every split shorter than the next copy plus the host work
leaves the GPU idle.

WHAT
----
The number of slots is N = 3 by default; GGML_SCHED_PREFETCH_SLOTS (2 to
4) overrides it. Prefetched splits use the slots in turn. The issue order
is unchanged: the copy for split k+1 is issued once split k is submitted
and waits for the slot's previous user, now split k-2, which has normally
finished. The copy starts at once and the scheduler thread blocks only for
the copy itself while the GPU works on splits k-1 and k. Every ordering
of 108 holds for any N: a copy waits for the compute of its slot's
previous user, which was submitted (and its compute_done recorded) at
least one split earlier, and each compute waits for its own copy. No new
wait is placed on the copy stream. GGML_SCHED_PREFETCH_SLOTS=2 restores
exactly the behaviour of patch 108 and is the knob for tight VRAM.

VRAM: N slots, each the largest one split's host weights: 210 MiB per
slot for Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, so the default third slot costs 210 MiB
more than patch 108 (630 instead of 420 MiB; 840 MiB with 4).
ggml_backend_sched_get_buffer_size and the reserve size count all N, so
memory fitting sees them.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 590, llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B
Q4_K_M, -ncmoe 99, HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1, ROC_CPU_WAIT_FOR_SIGNAL=1, tok/s):

| prompt / ubatch | 2 slots       | 3 slots       |
| --------------- | ------------- | ------------- |
| pp2048 / 2048   | 565.5, 565.2  | 598.7, 598.9  |
| pp512 / 512     | 282.2, 281.6  | 331.8, 330.9  |
| tg128           | 25.89, 26.22  | 26.07, 26.12  |

Host time per 2048-token batch: the scheduler thread's wait for the
slot's previous compute fell from 2024 to 1797 ms, the copy record stayed
at 1468 ms (the copies' own duration), and no compute had to wait for its
copy. With three slots the copies are hidden: the same batch with the
expert copies skipped (a timing-only build that computes on whatever the
slots hold) runs at 630 tok/s against 593, so the rest of the batch time
is GPU compute. Results are unchanged: llama-perplexity with 2 and 3 slots (512-token
batches, 8 chunks) gives the same perplexity at every chunk. 0 GPU faults.

Through Ollama (2,800-token prompt, two rounds, all experts placed by
llama.cpp's fit with the desktop's VRAM in use): prompts 492 / 487 ->
522 / 522 tok/s, generation 27.5 / 27.5 -> 27.8 / 27.6 tok/s. The fit sees
the larger compute buffer (866 -> 1077 MiB) and keeps about 290 MiB fewer
expert weights in VRAM; those experts then run on the CPU, whose kernels
round differently, so Qwen3.6's greedy text differs from a 2-slot run from
a near-tie on. Models whose weights all fit are unaffected (greedy output
identical on Qwen3 8B, Gemma 4 E2B and Gemma 3n E2B).

